 2. Trump&#x27;s AI co-chair defends flexible strategy 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Scott Kupor, co-chair of President Trump's "super intelligence" task force. Photo: Al Drago/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Maria Curi spoke yesterday with Scott Kupor, who is set to be a co-chair of President Trump's new "super intelligence" task force. 

 Trump last month announced what he called an "AI Force" on social media , sparking a flurry of questions about what AI regulation coming out of Washington will look like. Federal Trade Commission Chair Andrew Ferguson and Pentagon undersecretary Emil Michael will also co-chair the task force, while National Intelligence Director Jay Clayton leads as AI czar. Here's an edited and condensed version of the interview. 

 Is it an open question whether or not there will be an AI Force? 

 Kupor: To my knowledge, yes. I think Jay's view, which I agree with, was "Let's make sure we actually take the time," because the last thing we want to do is go into a hole for two years and come up with some big policy which will be outdated.

 At the White House AI lunch last week industry players represented varying perspectives. How do you reconcile all of those? 

 Kupor: I don't have a magic answer to that, other than, we're gonna have to listen to everybody. 

 How would you address the criticism that the administration is basically choosing not to regulate? 

 Kupor: I reject that concept. We have not come in and said: "There shall be a new piece of legislation." And so people think that's asleep at the switch. 

 What's next: Task force members and other government officials "who are relevant" will very likely have a kickoff meeting this week, Kupor said. 

